

⭐ CMB BLUEPRINT FILE LOADER (Final Form)

The Blueprint Loader has one job:

Load a .cmb file → extract structure → expose it to the viewer

That’s it.

Everything else is optional.

The loader does NOT:

validate physics

simulate anything

execute code

It simply parses, indexes, and maps blueprint content.

⭐ 1. Input → Output (The Entire Loader in One Line)

.blueprint file → { models, fields, organisms, meta_equation, notes }

The viewer uses this to:

highlight active sections

show blueprint snippets

link events to code

display HUD metadata

⭐ 2. File Structure (Minimal Assumption)

A .cmb file is plain text with blocks like:

universe { ... }
fields { ... }
models { ... }
organisms { ... }
meta_equation { ... }
notes { ... }

The loader doesn’t care about syntax beyond block boundaries.

⭐ 3. Loader Pipeline (Final Form)

read file
strip comments
find blocks
parse block headers
store block text
index by name
return blueprint object

That’s the entire pipeline.

⭐ 4. Block Extraction

The loader identifies blocks by simple patterns:

block_name {
    ...content...
}

Supported blocks:

fields

models

organisms

meta_equation

notes

Each block is stored as:

{
  "name": "models",
  "text": "...raw block text...",
  "start": line_number,
  "end": line_number
}

This gives the viewer:

scroll‑to‑block

highlight‑block

show‑snippet

⭐ 5. Model Indexing

Inside the models block, the loader extracts:

model_name → block_text

Example:

models {
  WARP { use: WarpTimeModel; }
  CLASSICAL { use: ClassicalModel; }
}

Viewer uses this to:

highlight the active model

show model parameters

link Ξ regime to blueprint code

⭐ 6. Organism Definitions

Inside organisms:

organism_name → block_text

Example:

organisms {
  warp_bubble { radius: dynamic; }
}

Viewer uses this to:

label organisms

show organism type

display lifecycle rules

⭐ 7. Meta‑Equation Block

This is the brain of the blueprint.

Loader extracts:

meta_equation → block_text

Viewer uses this to:

show meta‑equation snippet

highlight transitions

pulse on regime switches

⭐ 8. Notes Block (Optional)

Blueprint authors can embed notes:

@note "Warp ignition expected here"

Loader collects them into:

notes: [
  { line: 42, text: "Warp ignition expected here" }
]

Viewer uses this for:

timeline markers

HUD annotations

blueprint panel highlights

⭐ 9. Loader Output (Final Object)

The loader returns a simple object:

{
  "fields": {...},
  "models": {...},
  "organisms": {...},
  "meta_equation": "...",
  "notes": [...],
  "raw": "...entire file text..."
}

This is everything the viewer needs.



